      "How it works",
      "Every digital photo carries more data than the eye needs. Lossy compression — the kind used by JPG and WebP — works by discarding fine detail your visual system barely registers, especially in areas of subtle colour gradation. A quality slider controls the trade: higher quality keeps more data and a bigger file; lower quality throws more away and shrinks the file further.",
      "Browser-based compression re-encodes your image through the browser's own canvas engine, applying that quality setting and, where useful, converting the format. WebP, for instance, typically produces noticeably smaller files than JPG at similar visual quality, which is why many website owners convert their images as part of the process.",
      "PNG behaves differently because it is lossless: it preserves every pixel, which is right for logos, diagrams and screenshots with text, but makes photo-sized PNG files enormous. Converting a photographic PNG to JPG or WebP is usually the single biggest saving available — often the difference between a file that uploads and one that does not.",
